   "stem": {
      "text": "The integral test is applied to the series \\( \\sum_{n=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{n^{2}+4} \\). Take f(x) to be the series' general term with n replaced by x, and the lower limit of integration to be the series' starting index. Find the exact value of the improper integral of f from that lower limit to infinity."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Divide",
         "Pi",
         8
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Take \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{2}+4} \\), so \\( f(n) \\) is the general term. On \\( [2, \\infty) \\), \\( f \\) is positive, continuous, and decreasing, so the integral test applies.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            1,
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Power",
                  "x",
                  2
               ],
               4
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The series starts at \\( n = 2 \\), so the matching integral is \\( \\int_{2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{x^{2}+4} \\, dx = \\lim_{b \\to \\infty} \\int_{2}^{b} \\frac{1}{x^{2}+4} \\, dx \\).",
         "mathjson": 2
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "An antiderivative is \\( F(x) = \\frac{\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}}{2} \\), so \\( \\int_{2}^{b} \\frac{1}{x^{2}+4} \\, dx = \\frac{\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{b}{2} \\right)}}{2} - \\frac{\\pi}{8} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            [
               "Divide",
               [
                  "Arctan",
                  [
                     "Divide",
                     "b",
                     2
                  ]
               ],
               2
            ],
            [
               "Negate",
               [
                  "Divide",
                  "Pi",
                  8
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "As \\( b \\to \\infty \\), \\( F(b) \\to \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\), so the integral converges to \\( \\frac{\\pi}{8} \\). This number is the value of the integral, not the sum of the series.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            "Pi",
            8
         ]
      }
